Q1.  What is a Marker Interface? How is it different from a regular interface?
A Marker Interface is an interface that contains no methods or fields. It is used only to provide information (a "mark") to the JVM or
compiler that a class has a special property.

Examples: Serializable, Cloneable, Remote

Purpose
Indicates that a class possesses a particular capability.
JVM (complier) can perform special operations based on the marker.

Q2. Design a Java interface called Drawable with a default method defaultColor(). Implement this interface in two classes and show how 
one class can override the default method.
interface Drawable {
    default void defaultColor() {
        System.out.println("Default Color: Black");
    }

    void draw();
}

class Circle implements Drawable {
    public void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing Circle");
    }
}

class Rectangle implements Drawable {
    public void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing Rectangle");
    }

    @Override
    public void defaultColor() {
        System.out.println("Default Color: Blue");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Circle c = new Circle();
        Rectangle r = new Rectangle();

        c.draw();
        c.defaultColor();

        r.draw();
        r.defaultColor();
    }
}
OUTPUT--Drawing Circle
Default Color: Black
Drawing Rectangle
Default Color: Blue

Explanation:
Circle uses the interface's default implementation.
Rectangle overrides the default method and provides its own implementation.

